Linked Lists in Python

 Singly linked list | Each node points to the next node 
 Doubly linked list | Each node points to both the next and previous nodes 
 Circular singly linked list | The last node points back to the first node 
 Circular doubly linked list | Next and previous references form a circle 

1. What is a linked list?:A linked list is a sequence of objects called nodes
Each node contains:
A value
A reference to the next node

Example::
head
 |
[10 | next] -> [20 | next] -> [30 | None]

The first node contains 10 and points to the second node
The second node contains 20 and points to the third node
The third node contains 30 and points to None, which marks the end of the list

The Node class
Python does not provide a built-in general-purpose linked-list class, so we implement one manually

The basic node syntax is:
class Node:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value
        self.next = None

# Create three nodes:
first = Node(10)
second = Node(20)
third = Node(30)

Initially, the nodes are independent:
first  → [10 | None]
second → [20 | None]
third  → [30 | None]

Connect them:
first.next = second
second.next = third

Now the structure is:
first
  |
[10 | •] -> [20 | •] -> [30 | None]
The first node becomes the head:
head = first

4. What is head?: head stores a reference to the first node
head = first
The entire list can be reached through head: head → first → second → third

